<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · c. 290 · Egypt &amp; North Africa</p><h1 class="sg-title">Oxyrhynchus hymn</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The oldest Christian hymn found with its music written down, on a scrap of papyrus from Egypt about 1,700 years old.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Greek-speaking Christians of Roman Egypt</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Oxyrhynchus, Egypt</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>papyrus from around the end of the 3rd century AD</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words anonymous (papyrus of the late 3rd century AD))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxyrhynchus-hymn">Oxyrhynchus hymn</a>, catalogued as P. Oxy. XV 1786, is the earliest known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of a Christian hymn in Greek that preserves both its words and its musical notation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> dates from around the end of the 3rd century AD. It was found in 1918 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxyrhynchus">Oxyrhynchus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and published in 1922, and was kept in the Papyrology Rooms of the Sackler Library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, renamed the Bodleian Art, Archaeology and Ancient World Library in 2022.</p><p>The music is written in Greek vocal notation, the system of the classical Greek musicians who came before. The melody is entirely diatonic, spans exactly one octave and is notated in the Hypolydian; rhythmic signs mark the lengths of the notes. The words are set mostly one note to a syllable, with a few short melismas, in a largely anapaestic metre. That educated Greek-speaking Christians wrote their hymn in this notation shows them taking over the musical writing of their pagan predecessors.</p><p>The hymn is the only surviving fragment of notated Christian Greek music from the first four hundred years of the Christian era, although the historian and musician Kenneth Levy argued that the Sanctus melody best preserved in the Western medieval Requiem Mass goes back to around the 4th century. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phos-hilaron">Phos Hilaron</a>, it is one of the two earliest Christian Greek hymn texts reasonably certain to have been used in worship that are neither taken from the Bible nor modelled on biblical passages.</p><p>Ensembles that reconstruct ancient Greek music have recorded it, among them Atrium Musicæ de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> under Gregorio Paniagua (1979), Christodoulos Halaris (1992), Ensemble De Organographia (1995) and Ensemble Kérylos under Annie Bélis (1996). In 2025 it was adapted as a contemporary worship song in The First Hymn, a documentary project led by the Australian historian John Dickson, with an arrangement by Chris Tomlin and Ben Fielding based on the original text and melody.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn of praise to the Christian Trinity. It calls for silence across creation so that the praise may be heard, a picture of cosmic stillness that older Greek hymns also used.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Ancient Greek</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Ancient Greek</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">... ]ὁμοῦ, πᾶσαι τε Θεοῦ λόγιμοι δε …? ι … ? [… ὅσα κ[όσμος]</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">… ]homou, pasai te Theou logimoi de …? i … ? [… hosa k[osmos]</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>… together, all the eminent ones of God … [all that the] w[orld holds] …</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">... o]ὐ τὰν ἠῶ σιγάτω, μηδ᾿ ἄστρα φαεσφόρα χ[..]δε–</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">… o]u tan ēō sigatō, mēd' astra phaesphora ch[..]de–</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>… [neither night] nor dawn: let it be silent; let not the light-bearing stars [fall be-]</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">[σ]θων [ἐκ]λειπ[όντων] ῥ[ιπαὶ πνοιῶν, πηγαὶ] ποταμῶν ῥοθίων πᾶσαι. ὑμνούντων δ᾿ἡμῶν</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">[s]thōn [ek]leip[ontōn] rh[ipai pnoiōn, pēgai] potamōn rhothiōn pasai. hymnountōn d' hēmōn</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>[hind,] let the rushing winds and all the springs of roaring rivers cease. While we sing</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">[π]ατέρα χυἱὸν χἅγιον πνεῦμα, πᾶσαι δυνάμεις ἐπιφωνούντων ἀμὴν ἀμήν, κράτος αἶνος</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">[p]atera ch' hyion ch' hagion pneuma, pasai dynameis epiphōnountōn amēn amēn, kratos ainos</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of Father and Son and Holy Spirit, let all the powers answer: Amen, amen. Power, praise</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">[ἀεὶ καὶ δόξα Θεῷ] δ[ωτ]ῆ[ρι] μόνω[ι] [πάν]των ἀγαθῶν, ἀμὴν ἀμήν.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">[aei kai doxa Theōi] d[ōt]ē[ri] monō[i] [pan]tōn agathōn, amēn amēn.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>[always, and glory to God], the only giver of all good things. Amen, amen.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Papyrus Oxyrhynchus 1786 (late 3rd century), in Charles Cosgrove's edition (2011) as quoted in Wikipedia's article; square brackets mark letters restored by editors. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxyrhynchus_hymn" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (scientific transliteration of Ancient Greek (ISO 843 type)).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (3)</summary><ul><li>The beginning of the papyrus is lost, so the hymn starts mid-sentence. Square brackets mark letters and words restored by editors; dots and question marks, letters that cannot be read.</li><li>Lines 2–3: the scribe broke a word across the line (&quot;…de– / [s]thōn&quot;), so the English runs over too.</li><li>Above the words the papyrus carries the melody in Greek vocal notation: the oldest surviving Christian hymn with its music.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
